  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a single city, and the reliance on Baidu Heatmap data as a population proxy may introduce demographic biases toward smartphone users.",
      "what": "This study analyzes the spatial and temporal dynamics of human activity around rail transit stations in Tianjin, China, using hourly Baidu Heatmap data and a Geographically and Temporally Weighted Regression (GTWR) model. The analysis reveals that centrally located, commercially dense station areas experience the highest vitality, characterized by a bimodal daily pattern. Factors such as public service density, floor area ratio, and bus route integration show significant, spatially varying influences on local activity levels.",
      "why": "It shifts the focus of urban vitality research from traditional public spaces like parks and streets to transit nodes, providing a localized, hourly measurement of how specific built-environment features attract or deter people throughout the day."
    }
  },
